P-280: key the saved permission classification per descriptor

209,252 occurrences become 12,955 on the laboratory capture, and every
permission is still reachable.

After P-279 a judgement held one row per permission occurrence, each
carrying the object's distinguished name three times - in the row, in the
primary key and in the unique index. On the owner's forest that projects
to ~134 GB against 18.67 GB of evidence.

The classification of an entry is a function of the entry and the run's
catalogue and of nothing about the object carrying it. The 2026-09-24
review followed the tempting contextual cases - computer creation on the
Domain Controllers OU, WRITE_OWNER against OWNER RIGHTS, replication
combinations, callback and inherit-only entries, property sets - and
found no path from a target property to a verdict. So the occurrence now
says *this descriptor, at this ordinal, carries that entry*, and objects
reach their own rows through object_security_descriptor, which already
exists and already has exactly the rows needed. 940,067 production
objects carry 267,550 descriptors: 330,856,576 occurrences become
141,693,937.

THREE BINDINGS, NONE INFERRED FROM THE OTHERS

This is the review's central demand and the reason the table is shaped
this way rather than simply narrowed. Sharing a row between objects is
precisely how one object comes to be shown another object's permissions.

  1. object -> descriptor comes from the sealed EVIDENCE, through the
     ObjectPermissions.DescriptorId added in aee2961. The judgement also
     records it, and the read refuses when the two disagree. Recording
     and trusting is the defect the review named - "X uses D2" where
     evidence says D1 would validate every fingerprint, because whoever
     edited it recomputes them. Recording and comparing is a second
     witness, and it keeps Complete able to count occurrences per object.
  2. descriptor -> ordered entries is one fingerprint over the whole
     list. Per occurrence it would be 32 bytes x 141,693,937 = 4.5 GB in
     a change worth 6.9; over the list it is 267,550 x 32 = 8.5 MB and
     strictly stronger, because it binds the ordinals to each other.
     Mutation-tested with the entry count disabled beside it so the count
     could not stand in for it: both removal and reordering read back
     with no exception thrown.
  3. entry -> interpretation moves from the occurrence to the one row per
     distinct entry. P-283 created this binding; normalisation is what
     makes it urgent, because one wrong association now reaches every
     occurrence of that entry at once.

Also: position is gone - it was exactly zero-based rank by ordinal and is
recovered by ordering at read, removing a column and an index. Ordinal
gaps are kept; completeness is never inferred from contiguity, because a
truncated tail leaves a contiguous prefix. The occurrence is WITHOUT
ROWID on (descriptor_id, ordinal), so the key b-tree is the table.
security_descriptor.raw_bytes is untouched: it lives in sealed evidence,
and bumping BundleSchema.Version would make the owner's 18.67 GB capture
unreadable by the build meant to be tested against it.

Schema 46, checkpoint 06F6363E. Unlike 44 and 45 this one really does
move the DDL.

TWO THINGS FOUND WHILE MUTATION-TESTING, AND THEY ARE THE VALUE OF DOING IT

The write-side descriptor agreement check MEASURED NOTHING. Disabling it
left all sixty classification tests passing, because no case had two
objects claim one descriptor while carrying different entries - which
under per-descriptor keying is how up to 4,568 objects would silently
take one object's permissions. P280ADescriptorCannotBeClaimedTwice-
WithDifferentEntries was written for it and fails when it is disabled.

And a first draft narrowed the schema-support verification to run only
for entries whose interpretation was new. Two entries can name one schema
GUID and disagree about what it means; that is a fault in the run's
catalogue rather than in either entry, so it has to be caught wherever it
appears. Caught by ConflictingSupportWithinTransactionRollsBackEverything.

